  "cause": "A new entry, and the cause is not an event but a month of accumulation. **The protests began last month with students demanding better school conditions and escalated to take in fuel prices, the cost of living and deteriorating public services; on Saturday some 550,000 demonstrators took to the streets, largely peacefully, and police arrested about 1,800 people as groups — some dressed in black, masked and wearing balaclavas — rioted and looted in Paris, Marseille, Lille and other cities** (BBC). **Officials now say more than 2,500 people have been arrested and about 1,000 police officers injured; the interior ministry said more than 500 police, gendarmes and firefighters were hurt on Saturday alone; the Retail and Trade Federation counted about 1,500 shops and retailers damaged, while French reports put the number of looted businesses above 3,000; one demonstrator's hand was blown off by a police crowd-control grenade, according to AFP** (BBC, AFP). **In Marseille rioters broke into the city hall using a metal fence as a battering ram, a McDonald's was set on fire in Lille and an Apple store there broken into and looted, and at least one restaurant in Paris was gutted; a Paris resident told AFP \"it's like we had a war\", and another described looters \"proudly carrying supermarket baskets filled with goods and laughing about their thefts\"** (BBC, AFP). **The state's response has been curfew, arrest and a dispute about blame.** **Overnight curfews run from 23:00 to 06:00 in the Place de la République and the Les Halles area of central Paris, with a ban on unauthorised gatherings in several other parts of the centre, and partial curfews restricting young people and gatherings in Marseille, Bordeaux, Nice, Lyon and Lille; Interior Minister Laurent Nuñez — who offered his resignation on Sunday and had it refused by President Emmanuel Macron — admitted police had \"lost control\" in some cities, and the Élysée said he should be transparent about what Macron called \"intelligence and foresight failings\"** (BBC). **Prime Minister Sébastien Lecornu said mayors would be empowered to impose curfews \"where they need\", did not rule out a state of emergency, promised a law to make those who damaged property pay, and announced a partial unemployment fund for those unable to work; 200 investigators are analysing CCTV to identify ringleaders, and the president of the National Assembly, Yaël Braun-Pivet, has proposed a parliamentary inquiry into how the riots were organised** (BBC). **The explanation is contested: French intelligence had briefed government circles earlier this month that left-wing anarchist groups were involved in the student protests — a circled black A was spray-painted on buildings across the country — and an intelligence report dated 29 September, published by Le Figaro, described the \"manipulation\" of student demonstrations by student unions and movements aligned with the radical left, while French reports describe the mass looting as largely opportunistic** (BBC). *Whether this was organisation, opportunism, or a state that was not prepared is now the argument — and each answer points at a different remedy.*",
